From 729ea9fbc5a4939fb4d7cceb6b775b5d423485e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:30:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] l2r-local: step D (R2c): Mpz::is_shared through
 compiler-emitted glue from rcgmp.mpz.is_unique

lean2rr's dbgTraceIfShared (Lean's `lean_dbg_trace_if_shared`, natively
`!lean_is_scalar(a) && !lean_is_exclusive(a)`) must say whether a big Nat
or Int held in runtime code is shared. rcgmp's C API has no count or
uniqueness query; its dialect has `rcgmp.bridge.mpz.is_unique` (a heap
handle with an owned reference; false for an immortal).

Codegen emits a third function with the owned handle's acquire/release
glue, in the same place and the same way (home unit, `weak_odr`, hidden):
`__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_is_unique(handle) -> u8`, 1 for an immediate
(`rcgmp.bridge.mpz.is_immediate`) and otherwise `rcgmp.bridge.mpz.is_unique`.
It returns a byte rather than an `i1`, like the borrowed comparison
entries, so the C ABI value is defined. `Mpz::is_shared` is
`is_unique == 0`. So an immediate is never shared, a heap integer is
shared while another reference holds it, and an immortal literal always
is: natively, a persistent object (count 0) fails `lean_is_st`, so
`lean_is_exclusive` is false and dbgTraceIfShared reports it, as for Lean's
closed constants. The backend gains the `is_immediate` and `is_unique`
builders; reussir-core names the symbol (`RCGMP_IS_UNIQUE`).

Tests: frontend/bigint_ffi_shared.rr (an immediate, a unique heap value,
a heap value the caller still holds, one after a clone in the texture, an
immortal literal, a negative heap Int, and Vec<Nat> elements read while
the vector holds them; the glue's MLIR; run with the glue out of line and
inlined) and its ASan twin sanitizer/bigint-ffi-shared-asan.rr, the hidden
weak_odr definition in frontend/bigint_ffi_glue.rr,
docs/design/polymorphic-ffi.md.
